// What an array accessor hands back, for every command that carries one.
//
// The gap this closes was measured, not guessed: an accessor cut to hand its handler one element
// fewer than the guest sent replays both corpora at `cmds N/N` with the census unchanged and
// KosmicKrisp's own workload counters byte-identical. `venus-roundtrip` cannot see it either --
// it never calls an accessor. So the accessors are the one emitted surface with no witness, and
// one template mistake is a hundred identical bugs.
//
// Each test plants arrays into a command, encodes it with the real argument encoder, decodes it
// with the real decoder, and asks the accessor what it sees. That chain forces four emissions to
// agree -- planter, encoder, decoder, accessor -- and the round trip already pins the middle two
// against wire a C encoder produced, so what this adds on top is exactly the accessor.
//
// Two assertions, which are the accessor's only two degrees of freedom:
//
// - **Length.** The count the decoder sized its allocation from, and the count the accessor
//   builds its slice from, are two separate emissions of one expression.
// - **Which member.** The slice must start at the pointer member it names. Where several arrays
//   share a count, only this separates them, and where they also share an element type -- as
//   `pOffsets`, `pSizes` and `pStrides` do -- the type system does not.
//
// Contents are deliberately not asserted: they are the decoder's, and the round trip pins them
// over every recorded command. What is planted here is whatever `Default` gives, which for a
// handle array is `VK_NULL_HANDLE` -- an ordinary wire value that resolves without consulting
// the object table.
//
// Only arrays whose count is a plain member of the command are covered, because those are the
// ones a planter can establish both halves of. The rest are named in the module's own list
// below, so a reader can see what is not here rather than assume it is.

use super::serialize::*;
use super::types::*;
use crate::venus::cs::{AllOfIt, Decoder, Encoder, IdentityObjects};
use bumpalo::Bump;
use std::sync::atomic::AtomicBool;

/// One array's verdict: the slice the accessor handed back, against what was planted.
///
/// `None` is a failure here and not a question. Every array these tests plant is one the guest
/// sent, so an accessor that answers "the guest sent none" is answering about a different
/// command than the one that was encoded.
fn carried<T>(got: Option<&[T]>, want: usize, member: *const (), field: &str) {
    let slice = got.unwrap_or_else(|| panic!("{field}: the accessor says the guest sent no array"));
    assert_eq!(slice.len(), want, "{field}: the accessor's length is not the count on the wire");
    // Compared as bare addresses. An out-handle array's accessor hands out `Guest<T>` over a
    // member declared `*mut T`, and a blob's hands out `u8` over a `c_void` -- the element type
    // is the accessor's business, and what is being asked here is only which member it read.
    assert_eq!(
        slice.as_ptr() as *const (),
        member,
        "{field}: the accessor handed back a different member's array"
    );
}

/// How many elements every planted array gets.
///
/// More than one, so an accessor that hands back a single element or walks with the wrong stride
/// is caught, and small enough that a failure prints legibly. Odd, so a length halved or doubled
/// cannot land back on it.
const N: usize = 3;
